{-
   A 64-bit integer lane, which is a `bigint` on JS and a quadword lane natively - Design-Vector
   §7.3, and the trade that section now records as reversed.

   The lane was refused outright on JS until it wasn't. The argument for refusing it was sound and
   is still written down: a `bigint` is a heap value off the ordinary arithmetic path, so a vector
   of them clears no floor §7.2 asks for. What the refusal also did was take `Real(Vec(F64))` with
   it - every reinterpreting member of that instance reads a double through `Vec(Long)` - so the
   cost of the rule was a program that did not compile rather than one that ran slowly.

   **What this fixture asserts is that the two targets agree**, which is the property worth having
   and the one the representation makes easy to lose: every lane here is a `number` natively and a
   `bigint` under Node, and the answers have to be the same anyway. Three of the lines are here
   because they are the ones a `bigint` breaks and a `number` does not:

     - `shr` is a *logical* right shift, and `>>>` is not defined on BigInt at all. It goes through
       `BigInt.asUintN` first, exactly as the scalar path does.
     - `abs` is `Math.abs` at every other lane width, and `Math.abs` coerces to a number and throws
       on a bigint. A conditional is the magnitude there.
     - `horizontalMin`/`horizontalMax` are `Math.min`/`Math.max` elsewhere, and throw for the same
       reason. A comparison and a select is what they are here - total, since a bigint has no NaN.

   What is *not* here is the set of operations x64 refuses at this lane width and JS supports:
   `*`, `/`, `%`, `sar` and `abs` are all AVX-512 or expand-lane-by-lane on x86, and
   `unsupportedVectorReason` says so at the instruction. A fixture that runs on both targets can
   only assert their intersection, so `abs` is checked through a subtraction from zero instead.
-}

fn main() -> Int:
    let a = splat(7 :: Long) :: Vec(Long)
    let b = splat(3 :: Long) :: Vec(Long)
    let c = splat(0 - 9 :: Long) :: Vec(Long)
    let &n = 0 :: Long

    n = n + lane(a + b, 0)
    n = n + lane(a - b, 0)
    n = n + lane(a `and` b, 0)
    n = n + lane(a `or` b, 0)
    n = n + lane(a `xor` b, 0)
    n = n + lane(a `shl` b, 0)

    -- The logical shift, which is the one `>>>` cannot spell over a bigint.
    n = n + lane(a `shr` b, 0)

    -- The magnitude, written as the subtraction x64 can emit at this width.
    n = n + lane(0 - c, 0)

    -- The three reductions, whose extremum is a comparison here rather than a `Math` call.
    n = n + horizontalSum(a)
    n = n + horizontalMax(a)
    n = n + horizontalMin(c)

    -- A value past 2^32, which is the whole reason the lane is 64 bits: it survives every one of
    -- the operations above as itself rather than as its low half.
    let wide = splat(4294967296 :: Long) :: Vec(Long)
    n = n + lane(wide `shr` splat(32 :: Long), 0)

    return truncate(n) :: Int
